 and finally Poland do mean  that there's a broad public feeling that  the world is dangerous and we have to be  capable of surviving in it I think  that's where wrap grab significant well  I think the actions taken by the  president with regard to the Soviet  Union after Afghanistan were a departure  in public policy to some extent from  what we've done in the past it was an  exertion of linkage if you will in the  Soviet relationship it was an attempt to  treat their bad behavior with punishment  I think it had an effect will it stop  them from going into Poland probably not  but did it have an effect I think it did  and I think it was a constructive effect  and I think we all however agree that  having sufficient military power as one  of our foreign policy tools is  absolutely necessary and that since  Vietnam whether we like it or not we've  allowed that military machine to fall  into a certain state of disrepair which  has to be remedied all right I think  that we have opened up this whole issue  of the possibilities of future we've  certainly decided the possibilities for  revitalizing America are numerous but  that the achievement of the goals is  going to be a hard road and it's time  for us to have a question-and-answer  session may I have the first question  please I'm Howard pennyman and I'm from  American Enterprise Institute in  Georgetown University could I go back to  some of the very early discussion the  question of whether people represent  their district or represent the United  States is an old question Woodrow Wilson  said as far as that century ago that  American congressman represented their  district and not the nation as a whole  every basic reform whether it be to  change the electoral system or to reform  the Congress has tended to further  fragment and decentralize the Congress  one of the results is it  to me and I'd like to ask all of you to  comment on it is that it has so made  congressman represent their districts  that they both a 