ction  they specifically want us to speak for  the district and they have the rather  naive belief that if a president can't  find 218 districts that gain more than  they lose from a particular bill that  that bill that will be defeated now I  think by George you're supposed to speak  for the people of your district because  that's who you understand that's who you  represent and that you collectively as  the house represent the country and I  think any congressman who gets up and  says well this will destroy my district  economically but in the national  interest I want to do that is in fact  betraying their specific commitment in  the Constitution and I find it very  frankly naive and destructive in the  long run to keep favoring a general good  which has no specific good because I  don't think there is a general good for  the country as a whole which you can't  piece together in 218 districts but Newt  wouldn't you agree then on that basis  and I think this goes back to to the  question that was asked that given the  constitutional arrangement given our  mandate as congressman to represent our  districts that in looking for that spark  that dynamic spark or leadership or  initiative that is going to set the  agenda and helps all the problems we  have to look to the presidency that that  is more likely to be the arena in which  the answers are found than in the  Congress specifically and precisely in  the Federalist Papers and in almost all  the writing the founding fathers a  concern that we have effective  leadership in the Congress because it is  among the congressmen that you should  make the compromises and the  understandings and the level of  political sophistication necessary both  to manage the people who we pay the  bureaucrats and to rearrange the  legislation for our constituents and it  is the failure of our generation to  produce effective political and  congressional leaders in  Congress that leads us I think to the  very grave danger in the long run of  tyranny we would be most interested to  hear from you thank you Miss Bingley  attorney well I think it may be naive  and I will admit to that but to to  respond to mr. Gingrich what we need in  the United States Congress are men and  women who are not afraid to lose who do  not see this as a lifelong profession to  serve this a constituency so so readily  and only that constituency in every  little whim of public policy that the  breeze may blow to simply perpetuate  them in office now I know that it's a  lot easier to say than to do if they're  going to close a military base in your  district to build a more modern one  somewhere else  that is in the national interest to do  so it is not in the economic interest of  that members district that's when you  earn your pay to vote to close it in  your district in order to achieve the  national interest to modernize your  defense establishment I mean these  questions have been debated but we are  not 535 men and women are not city  councilmen we're not state legislatures  we shouldn't be Ward politicians  catering to whatever sense of  information and purpose that comes on a  weekly basis or you will never mold any  national policy but the problem is that  we won't stay here so most of us are  afraid to vote on any given issue in  something that's too brave because then  we get thrown out next time can I go  back on it I think the question is  perhaps not as well put as it could be  most issues don't break down very neatly  as I think dick said into a district  interest in an ash  interest it breaks down between what you  perceive as being both in the interest  of your district in and the national  interest and an interest group that is  usually represented in your constituency  let's take the example of the twice a  year Cola for federal retirees the  cost-of-living adjustment I think most  members probably felt that that was a a  change making it a once a year  cost-of-living adjustment was a change  that was in 