tem that you get the kind of  government that you deserve if you  tolerate apathy and your public  officials you get apathy if you tolerate  corruption then you get corruption  because we are still a government by the  government by those students and the  people in this room and people  throughout the country the Congress will  never be any greater in its  statesmanship than the values and the  hopes and the demands of the American  people we have the same faults they are  certain because we are representative  and come from a system where we are  elected every two years we mirror those  hopes and those aspirations and those  faults that means you get a certain  number of blue-eyed people you get a  certain number of alcoholics you get a  certain number of any other trend that  you want to put on your computer but  back to the question that we discussed  before the real question is in a  pluralistic society where you've got  black people and Spaniards and rich  people and poor people how on any given  vote can you be truly representative I  represent a district that's 50% black  and 50% white a district where the gap  between the number of high income people  a number of low income people that gap  in between is greater than any other  congressional district in the country  according to all these computer  operators so the question that I would  ask to your students is how can you be  on any  vote truly representative you can't I  know that on any given vote I'm not  going to make 50% of them mad but  probably 80% of them man when I cast a  vote that I deemed to be in the national  interest but you have to turn this  question back in a free society to the  people who are the electorate because  they are the government in this country  and again they're going to get what they  deserve in their representatives in  Washington sorry I think Congress itself  has to bear a great deal of the blame  for the way government's perceived  because we probably do as much  communicating as anybody in America  today next to the Walter Cronkite and  the people on this on the evening news  shows we send out newsletters we make  speeches we visit with our constituents  to the extent that the American people  have a perspective on government I think  to some extent we're responsible for  having created that we created by  castigating the courts and condemning  the bureaucracy and worrying about  alleged abuses of presidential power by  presidents and we also I think have  failed in a very important perspective  being reflective about what our role in  our responsibilities is yes we do have  to be representative yes we do have to  pass appropriations bills and conduct  oversight but we don't debate the great  issues of the day we spend hours and  hours and hours on absolutely idiotic  procedural questions we don't get into  the basic fundamental issues that I  think the American people often feel  ought to be the central concern of their  government and to some extent we brought  it on ourselves thank your students are  probably basically right I think that  there are two instances or two  conditions which will change the  condition of Congress one is if more of  your students will take it upon  themselves to run for Congress rather  than going into the business world to  make a big buck or secondly if we have a  catastrophe in the country and people  really begin to worry about what  Congress is doing is Dick said 95% of us  get reelected if we're not exerting  leadership  and the American people don't want  leadership because they're reelecting  people that your students they are not  giving them leadership I think as long  as things were going along relatively  well and they have been for 40 or 50  years up until about the last ten nobody  really cared and coming except for the  cadet you both you because you're both  Democrats and just went through a tough  election in terms of your party at large  it seems to me that one of our great  problems is that that each of us is  individually perceived as a very  effective representative that the  problem is that Congress in the  aggregate is not able to function in  with some anti synergism whatever that  word is that we bring together each of  the 435 parts and we somehow become less  than the sum of the whole and I think  that's been one of our great problems  we're all much better as individuals  than we are as a team you know I run  around my district and I looked at this  high school that I used to go to the  public high schools I couldn't figure  out how it was different it looked  different I could tell I couldn't it  took me 20 minutes of looking at it and  all of a sudden I realized that it was  this one tremendous parking lot around  it we didn't have cars 20 years ago when  I went to high school now everybody's  got their own car so when your students  start worrying about whether or not why  we don't have an effective energy policy  in this country ask them how many of  them would be willing to be statesmen  and give up those cars until we got our  imported oil bill down from ninety  billion dollars a year to ten million  dollars a year and we'll test  statesmanship at both ends but why  scheisse I sense that your students are  asking for tough leadership decisions  like a gas tax John Anderson was very  popular on college campuses he talked  about tough solutions to tough problems  and I think what you're saying is that  that's what they're asking for and I  would just suggest back that that will  happen when either we have a catastrophe  and then people demand those kinds of  things happen or will  to have a consensus form in that in that  regard or until we get 219 people in the  house and 51 people in the Senate who  perceived that that kind of thing I'm  not saying that's necessarily a right  policy but those kinds of things are  necessary to do should go ahead and  endorse my policy on the far the gas tax  I voted for at one point final remark  the students want to believe I think  that is an interesting point enrollments  in political science are up 50 60  percent over the last two years and the  interest in governance and governmental  institutions is truly growing and  Anderson's appeal was very substantial  around altruistic symbols I think the  complaint about Congress is they they  don't find inspiration they don't find  something there that they can look to as  more than a mirror of the society maybe  it can't be more than that but that's  that's somehow what they want anybody  else wants to talk to this what if in  fact the reason we are floundering  around in the Congress is because the  country is floundering around what if in  fact it is the nature of a free society  to take long periods to talk to itself  to sort things out to move slowly so as  not to crush any single group and that  one of the things that our students are  going to have to learn in the long run  is that to be free there's on occasion  to be impatient to be frustrated and  have to work awfully hard to get  anything done because that is in fact  precisely what freedom takes I think in  that sense if you look at our record  compared to Nazi Germany or compared to  Soviet Russia we don't do all that bad a  job over 20 years although certainly we  do a terrible job over one year all  right this concludes another public  policy forum presented by the American  Enterprise Institute for Public Policy  Research on behalf of AEI our hearty  thanks to the distinguished and expert  panelists congressman Newt Gingrich  Richard bee trainee Richard Gephardt and  wife Ella and of course also to our  guests at experts in the audience for  their participation  it is the aim of AEI to clarify issues  of the day by presenting many viewpoints  in the hope that by doing so those who  wish to learn about the decision-making  process will benefit from such a free  exchange of informed and enlightened  opinion this public policy forum series  is c